    Based on the search results, I found several key resources related to Baker, Norine, and the Riemann-Roch theorem for graphs and tropical curves:
    
    ## Main Results
    
    Baker and Norine proved a Riemann-Roch theorem for finite graphs, with results later extended to metric graphs to establish a Riemann-Roch theorem for divisors on abstract tropical curves.
    
    The foundational work appears in a 2007 paper: Matthew Baker and Serguei Norine published "Riemann–Roch and Abel–Jacobi theory on a finite graph" in Advances in Mathematics, 215(2):766–788, 2007.
    
    ## Related Work
    
    An elementary proof of the Riemann-Roch theorem for tropical curves, similar to the recent proof of the Riemann-Roch theorem for graphs by Baker and Norine, was presented by Hladký, Král, and Norine.
    
    The results and arguments employed by Norine and Baker were independently used by Gathmann and Kerber to formulate and prove a Riemann-Roch theorem in tropical geometry.
    
    ## Impact and Significance
    
    Baker and Norine introduced the rank of a divisor on a graph in close analogy with the rank of a divisor on an algebraic curve, and proved that it satisfies a precise analog of the Riemann–Roch Theorem.
    
    
