    # Baker-Norine 2007: Riemann-Roch and Abel-Jacobi Theory on Finite Graphs
    
    **arXiv Number:** math/0608360
    
    ## Overview
    Baker and Norine extend classical algebraic geometry to discrete settings, establishing that "a finite graph can be viewed, in many respects, as a discrete analogue of a Riemann surface."
    
    ## Key Contributions
    
    **Main Theorem:** The authors formulate and prove a graph-theoretic version of the classical Riemann-Roch theorem, connecting divisor theory on graphs to that on Riemann surfaces.
    
    **Divisor Rank:** The framework develops linear equivalence of divisors on graphs, establishing rank functions that parallel classical divisor rank theory.
    
    **Chip-Firing Game Connection:** A significant application characterizes winning strategies in chip-firing games (also called dollar games) played on graph vertices through the divisor rank framework—connecting combinatorial game theory to algebraic geometry.
    
    **Jacobian Theory:** The paper proves results about the Abel-Jacobi map from graphs to their Jacobian varieties, mirroring classical properties from Riemann surface theory.
    
    ## Impact
    This 35-page work (v3, published in *Advances in Mathematics*) opened productive research directions linking graph theory, combinatorics, and algebraic geometry, making classical geometric concepts computationally accessible on discrete structures.
